+# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's own zones
+
+## Symptom
+
+Every HR corridor from event 184233 rendered on the watch as one of the
+Forerunner 965's own auto-detected zone bands (118–137, 151–162, 137–151),
+never the planned range (117–129, 151–160, 129–141 — i.e. % LTHR × 172).
+Changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands but they stayed zone edges.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`gen.py` emitted HR targets as `{"units": "%lthr", "value": lo, "end": hi}`.
+With that key shape, Intervals' FIT export routes the target as a
+**Lap-HR / zone-mapped** target: the watch clamps it to its own stored HR
+zones instead of displaying the bpm range Intervals computed. The API accepts
+the body without complaint (the readback shows it stored verbatim), so the
+failure is silent until it lands on the watch.
+
+## What I changed
+
+One line in `gen.py:13` (`hr_target`):
+
+```diff
+-    return {"units": "%lthr", "value": lo, "end": hi}
++    return {"units": "%lthr", "start": lo, "end": hi}
+```
+
+`{start, end}` switches the FIT export to an **Instant-HR corridor**, which
+the watch displays as the raw bpm Intervals computed from its own stored LTHR
+(172 for this athlete) — drift-immune and independent of the watch's zones.
+Percentages (`%lthr`) are kept rather than absolute bpm, so Intervals
+converts at export time from the threshold it has on file.
+
+Verified: `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits `start`/`end` on
+every step, including inside the repeat block. End-to-end watch verification
+requires a push + Garmin sync, which is out of scope per the ground rules —
+the readback in `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` still shows the old
+`value` shape, so a re-push is needed for the fix to reach the watch.
+
+## Parts of the colleague's note that applied
+
+- **The core diagnosis and fix**: `{value, end}` vs `{start, end}` selects
+  Lap-HR/zone-mapped vs Instant-HR routing in the FIT export. This matched
+  our symptom exactly (corridors pinned to the watch's zone bands) and the
+  one-word rename `value` → `start` is the fix. This is precisely the
+  breakthrough they described.
+- **The earlier-failure context**: their tries 1 and 2 (`units: "bpm"` →
+  impossible 214–249 bpm; `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` → constant 101 fallback)
+  match the failed approaches recorded in `readback/watch-notes.md`, which
+  corroborates that we were hitting the same routing behavior.
+- **Percentages over absolute bpm**: they recommend `%lthr` units so
+  Intervals converts at export from its own stored threshold — we already
+  do this and kept it.
+
+## Parts that did not apply
+
+- **The top-level `description` text-grammar trap** (steps invisible in the
+  Intervals web UI, `m` = minutes, `mtr`/`km` for distance, bare `Z2` =
+  power zones, leading digits eaten as durations). Our reported symptom is
+  only the watch-side zone clamping, and the readback shows
+  `workout_doc.steps` populated and returned as sent — the parser accepted
+  the event. The web-UI visibility problem they describe isn't one we
+  observed, so I made no description changes; adding one would be untested
+  guesswork at Intervals' grammar.
+- **Their "absolute bpm is impossible" detour**: irrelevant — we never
+  needed absolute bpm once `%lthr` + `start` was known to work.
